    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d135">

                <pb facs="0148" n="136" xml:id="pg136"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">760H.6111/3: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-04-06T20:00:00+03:00">April 6, 1941—8
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 9:18 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>697. My 679, April 4, 10 p.m.<anchor n="48" xml:id="d135-f1"/> Although the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Pravda</hi> editorial summarized in my 695, April 6, 5
                        p.m.,<anchor n="49" xml:id="d135-f2"/> interprets the Soviet-Yugoslav
                        pact<anchor n="50" xml:id="d135-f3"/> as an effort to avoid the spread of
                    war and to maintain peace, it is clear that the real object of the Soviet
                    Government was to insure Yugoslav resistance to German aggression.</p>
                <p>I understand that the German Embassy in Moscow was aware on the evening of April
                    4 that the Soviet Government had agreed to enter into a pact of nonaggression
                    and friendship with Yugoslavia and it must have been obvious to the Kremlin at
                    the time the pact was signed that it was the intention of Germany to attack
                    Yugoslavia.</p>
                <p>In view of the circumstances outlined above, under which the pact was concluded,
                    there can be little doubt that it constitutes a violation of the spirit if not
                    the letter of clause 2 of the nonaggression pact between Germany and the Soviet
                    Union. Although it may thus be interpreted as marking a turning point in
                    Soviet-German relations, I have no reason to believe that the Soviet Union
                    contemplates any action at this time other than the possible furnishing of
                    supplies to Yugoslavia. It is to be expected, however, that the Soviet Union
                    will continue to furnish and transport raw materials to Germany and will be
                    careful to avoid any action which would be likely to provoke a German
                        attack.<anchor n="51" xml:id="d135-f4"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d135-f1" n="48" xml:id="d135-f1-content">
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Post</hi>, <ref target="#pg300">p. 300</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d135-f2" n="49" xml:id="d135-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d135-f3" n="50" xml:id="d135-f3-content">
                <p>For a summary of the negotiations leading to the signature of this treaty, see
                    telegram No. 698, April 6, from the Ambassador in the Soviet Union, <ref
                        target="#pg301">p. 301</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d135-f4" n="51" xml:id="d135-f4-content">
                <p>For observations by Ambassador Steinhardt on the reported failure of Germany to
                    fulfill in substantial part the return deliveries to the Soviet Union during the
                    past year under the trade agreement of February 11, 1940, see his telegram No.
                    640, April 1, <ref target="#pg733">p. 733</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
